WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:00.927
Thirteen years...

00:00:00.927 --> 00:00:02.744
That's how long we've waited.

00:00:04.500 --> 00:00:05.416
But here's the question...

00:00:05.416 --> 00:00:07.476
what the hell was Rockstar doing all this time?

00:00:08.500 --> 00:00:10.224
Was Rockstar just making a bigger map?

00:00:10.224 --> 00:00:10.716
Better graphics?

00:00:10.716 --> 00:00:14.164
Or were they trying to build something that had never existed in gaming before?

00:00:16.500 --> 00:00:19.410
Believe it or not, the story goes back to 2015.

00:00:19.410 --> 00:00:21.156
Rockstar developers were already visiting Miami,

00:00:21.156 --> 00:00:23.484
researching the city for their next massive project.

00:00:24.500 --> 00:00:25.899
They called it Project Americas.

00:00:25.899 --> 00:00:27.297
The original ambition was enormous...

00:00:27.297 --> 00:00:28.976
parts of North and South America.

00:00:28.976 --> 00:00:32.612
But eventually, Rockstar narrowed the world down to Miami and its surrounding areas.

00:00:32.762 --> 00:00:34.162
But there was a problem.

00:00:34.162 --> 00:00:36.122
Rockstar had another massive game to finish.

00:00:36.122 --> 00:00:38.642
Red Dead Redemption 2 was consuming the company's attention...

00:00:38.642 --> 00:00:40.322
and GTA 6 had to wait.

00:00:40.500 --> 00:00:43.708
Rockstar eventually moved much of its workforce toward finishing Red Dead 2.

00:00:43.708 --> 00:00:45.847
That pushed GTA 6 back by several years.

00:00:45.847 --> 00:00:47.985
And when Red Dead finally launched in 2018...

00:00:47.985 --> 00:00:50.124
the next era of GTA could move forward.

00:00:50.274 --> 00:00:51.793
Now, here's where things get interesting.

00:00:51.793 --> 00:00:54.326
Rockstar wasn't just trying to make a city look beautiful.

00:00:54.326 --> 00:00:56.099
They wanted the world to feel alive...

00:00:56.099 --> 00:00:57.618
even when you weren't doing anything.

00:00:57.768 --> 00:00:58.894
Developers studied real Miami.

00:00:58.894 --> 00:01:00.021
They rode with police,

00:01:00.021 --> 00:01:01.429
photographed the city from helicopters,

00:01:01.429 --> 00:01:03.682
visited nightclubs, and explored different parts of Florida.

00:01:03.682 --> 00:01:06.216
They were studying how the real world actually behaves.

00:01:06.366 --> 00:01:07.969
And the details go crazy.

00:01:07.969 --> 00:01:10.213
According to Rockstar's own demonstrations to Bloomberg,

00:01:10.213 --> 00:01:11.495
even small things matter...

00:01:11.495 --> 00:01:12.457
how Jason eats,

00:01:12.457 --> 00:01:14.060
how drinks move inside bottles,

00:01:14.060 --> 00:01:15.342
and how pedestrians behave.

00:01:15.492 --> 00:01:17.631
But perhaps the biggest challenge wasn't the city.

00:01:17.631 --> 00:01:19.771
It was the two people living inside it.

00:01:19.771 --> 00:01:22.980
Jason and Lucia weren't designed to feel like strangers sharing a game.

00:01:23.130 --> 00:01:24.908
Rockstar wanted them to interact naturally.

00:01:24.908 --> 00:01:27.278
They could text, meet up, spend time together,

00:01:27.278 --> 00:01:29.944
work out, rob places, and react to each other.

00:01:29.944 --> 00:01:32.610
Making all of that work required incredibly complex systems.

00:01:32.760 --> 00:01:33.872
Then came another problem.

00:01:33.872 --> 00:01:37.764
Rockstar realized the number of people it needed to write and animate was overwhelming.

00:01:37.764 --> 00:01:39.432
The projections were described as horrifying.

00:01:39.582 --> 00:01:42.161
So Rockstar built a dedicated studio in Los Angeles,

00:01:42.161 --> 00:01:45.026
with writers, producers and directors focused on creating those characters.

00:01:45.026 --> 00:01:48.750
Imagine building an entire team just to make the city's pedestrians feel believable.

00:01:48.900 --> 00:01:50.256
And then 2020 happened.

00:01:50.256 --> 00:01:51.950
The pandemic forced developers home,

00:01:51.950 --> 00:01:55.339
disrupted production, and even stopped performance-capture work for a period.

00:01:55.339 --> 00:01:57.372
The project faced another major obstacle.

00:01:57.522 --> 00:01:59.497
But the delays weren't only about technology.

00:01:59.497 --> 00:02:02.601
After Red Dead 2, Rockstar also faced criticism over excessive overtime.

00:02:02.601 --> 00:02:05.705
The company wanted to change how it made its next game...

00:02:05.705 --> 00:02:06.834
without repeating those mistakes.

00:02:09.500 --> 00:02:11.010
Look at the scale.

00:02:11.010 --> 00:02:14.408
Rockstar had 2,441 employees when Red Dead 2 launched.

00:02:14.408 --> 00:02:19.316
By August 2026, that number had grown to 4,711 people across twelve offices.

00:02:19.466 --> 00:02:22.120
Rockstar originally expected to release the game in 2025.

00:02:22.120 --> 00:02:23.299
Then came two delays.

00:02:23.299 --> 00:02:24.479
First to May 2026...

00:02:24.479 --> 00:02:25.953
and finally to November 19.

00:02:25.953 --> 00:02:29.786
The company said it needed more time to deliver the quality players expected.

00:02:29.936 --> 00:02:34.016
So maybe the answer isn't that Rockstar spent thirteen years making one video game.

00:02:34.016 --> 00:02:36.347
It spent more than eight years developing it...

00:02:36.347 --> 00:02:40.136
while rebuilding its team, changing its production process, and pushing its ambition further.

00:02:40.286 --> 00:02:41.915
And now, after all those years,

00:02:41.915 --> 00:02:45.988
Rockstar is about to put that entire world into the hands of millions of players.

00:02:45.988 --> 00:02:47.889
Every street, every character, every little detail...

00:02:47.889 --> 00:02:49.790
is about to face its biggest test.

00:02:49.940 --> 00:02:52.109
But here's what I really want to know...

00:02:52.109 --> 00:02:53.464
after thirteen years of waiting...

00:02:53.464 --> 00:02:55.362
will GTA 6 actually change gaming forever...

00:02:55.362 --> 00:02:57.260
or have we been expecting too much?

